% ============================================================================
% seismic-wave-arrival-order — the two named seismic body waves and which
% one an earthquake sends out first (adj-facts-stdlib, EARTH SCIENCE).
% ============================================================================
% An earthquake does not shake a distant recording station all at once — it
% sends out more than one kind of wave, and they arrive in a fixed order. A
% P wave gets there first; an S wave follows. Recall is a binding query:
%
%     ? seismic_wave_arrival_order(p_wave, $D)
%       % are_the_first_waves_to_arrive_after_an_earthquake
%
% A native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5): each `row` lowers to a ground relation
% `seismic_wave_arrival_order(wave, description)` carrying the source
% citation, so the lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding query — the
% engine returns the description WITH its source, on the CPU, with zero
% model calls, and abstains on a seismic-wave-related term that is not one
% of these two. The query also runs BACKWARD — bind a description and
% recall which wave it names:
%
%     ? seismic_wave_arrival_order($W, are_the_next_waves_to_arrive_after_p_waves)
%       % s_wave
%
% The two waves, as a little truth table:
%
%     wave      description                                              quote (verbatim)
%     --------  -------------------------------------------------------  -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
%     p_wave     are_the_first_waves_to_arrive_after_an_earthquake         "P-waves, or Primary waves, are the first waves to arrive as soon as there's an earthquake."
%     s_wave     are_the_next_waves_to_arrive_after_p_waves                "S-waves, or Secondary waves, are the next waves to arrive after P-waves."
%
% Provenance: quoted verbatim from Cal OES (California Governor's Office of
% Emergency Services) News' "What Are P-Waves and S-Waves?" article, which
% gives each wave its own standalone, parallel-worded defining sentence
% under its own subsection heading. WebFetch-verified across two passes
% before writing, the second pass pulling the full paragraph under each
% subsection heading to confirm the target sentence stands alone as a
% complete, single-fact definition and is not itself qualified by an
% adjacent sentence (each subsection's OWN second and third sentences —
% travel speed, felt-shaking intensity — are separate elaboration, not
% folded into the quoted definition). `trust authoritative` — Cal OES is a
% California state government (.gov) agency, the primary tier this stdlib
% already reserves for other government sources (e.g. NASA, USGS, NOAA).
%
% A third wave commonly grouped alongside these two is deliberately NOT a
% row here, so a recall ABSTAINS on it rather than inventing a description:
% `surface_wave`. It is a real seismic wave category, but every source
% checked either bundles its definition together with a second distinct
% fact in the same sentence (USGS: "P waves travel through solid and
% liquid, but S waves do not travel through liquid" contrasts two waves in
% one sentence; MTU: "Surface waves travel more slowly ... and are
% predominantly lower frequency" bundles speed and frequency), or defines
% it via a different framing dimension (Wikipedia's general physics
% definition, not the same earthquake-arrival-order framing the two tabled
% here share) rather than stating one clean fact in the same parallel
% pattern. Picked using the mandatory full-tree-grep-before-scoping
% discipline — zero hits for "seismic_wave"/"p_wave"/"s_wave" before this
% file was written; distinct from the sibling `physics/wave-types.adj`,
% which classifies waves into the mechanical/electromagnetic FAMILY axis
% (and explicitly abstains on seismic waves itself), not this arrival-order
% axis. (See ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table seismic_wave_arrival_order {
    columns wave, description

    row (p_wave, are_the_first_waves_to_arrive_after_an_earthquake)
    row (s_wave, are_the_next_waves_to_arrive_after_p_waves)

    source "P-waves, or Primary waves, are the first waves to arrive as soon as there's an earthquake."
    locator "https://www.news.caloes.ca.gov/what-are-p-waves-and-s-waves/"
    trust authoritative
}
